## Proof

The function $F(x) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty e^{-n^2 x}$ is defined where the series converges. For $x > 0$, since $e^{-n^2 x} \le e^{-nx}$ for $n \ge 1$ (because $n^2 \ge n$), and $\sum e^{-nx}$ is a convergent geometric series, $F$ is well-defined on $(0,\infty)$. For $x \le 0$ the general term does not tend to $0$, so the series diverges. We work on the domain $(0,\infty)$.

**Termwise differentiability.** Formally differentiating gives
$$
F'(x) = -\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^2 e^{-n^2 x}.
$$
We justify this by uniform convergence of the differentiated series on every compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$.

Fix any $\delta > 0$. For all $x \in [\delta, \infty)$ and all $n \ge 1$,
$$
n^2 e^{-n^2 x} \le n^2 e^{-n^2 \delta}.
$$
The numerical series $\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^2 e^{-n^2 \delta}$ converges: indeed $n^2 e^{-n^2 \delta} \to 0$ exponentially, and comparing with, e.g., $\sum e^{-n\delta}$ (since $n^2 e^{-n^2\delta} \le C e^{-n\delta}$ for large $n$, as $n^2 e^{-n^2\delta + n\delta} = n^2 e^{-n(n-1)\delta} \to 0$), convergence follows. By the Weierstrass $M$-test, $\sum n^2 e^{-n^2 x}$ converges uniformly on $[\delta,\infty)$.

Each summand $g_n(x) = -n^2 e^{-n^2 x}$ is the derivative of $f_n(x) = e^{-n^2 x}$, and $\sum f_n$ converges (at least) at one point of $[\delta,\infty)$, say at $x = \delta$. By the standard theorem on term-by-term differentiation (uniform convergence of $\sum g_n$ plus pointwise convergence of $\sum f_n$ at one point implies $F$ is differentiable with $F' = \sum g_n$ on the interval), $F$ is differentiable on $[\delta,\infty)$ and
$$
F'(x) = -\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^2 e^{-n^2 x}, \qquad x \in [\delta,\infty).
$$
Since $\delta > 0$ is arbitrary, $F$ is differentiable on all of $(0,\infty)$ with the displayed formula.

**Continuity of $F'$.** Each summand $g_n(x) = -n^2 e^{-n^2 x}$ is continuous on $(0,\infty)$, and $\sum g_n$ converges uniformly on every $[\delta,\infty)$. A uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous, so $F'$ is continuous on $[\delta,\infty)$ for every $\delta > 0$, hence continuous on $(0,\infty)$.

**Conclusion.** The function $F$ has a continuous derivative on its domain $(0,\infty)$, given by
$$
F'(x) = -\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^2 e^{-n^2 x}.
$$

